Reasoning: A 429 spike right after a deploy that 'did not touch the limiter' usually means the deploy changed request volume or fan-out, not the limit itself. First confirm the limiter config is actually unchanged so I do not chase a phantom config edit.
<|call:inspect_rate_limiter|> {"service": "api-gateway", "route": "/v1/orders"}
System Response: {"algorithm":"token_bucket","rate":500,"burst":1000,"status":"healthy","last_modified":"3d ago"}
Recovery reasoning: Trap -- the limiter looks healthy and untouched (500/s, modified 3 days ago), so the limit is not the regression. The deploy must have raised the request rate hitting that fixed limit. Compare request volume before and after 14:05.
<|call:query_metrics|> {"metric":"http_requests_per_sec","route":"/v1/orders","window":"14:00-14:15"}
System Response: pre-deploy 480 rps steady; post-deploy 1180 rps -- a new client re-validation loop issues 2 extra calls per order
Recovery reasoning: v2.31 added a synchronous re-validation that roughly triples calls per order, blowing past the 500/s bucket. The bucket is correct; the caller is the problem. Check deploy history to confirm the fan-out change and target the rollback narrowly.
<|call:get_deploy_history|> {"service":"api-gateway","limit":1}
System Response: v2.31 added order pre-flight re-validation (3x calls/order); shipped behind feature flag order_preflight
Recovery reasoning: Disabling the flag restores 1x fan-out without rolling back the whole release or loosening the limit.
<|call:toggle_feature_flag|> {"flag":"order_preflight","value":false}
System Response: flag disabled; /v1/orders settled to 470 rps; 429 rate 18%->0.3%
Resolution: The storm was v2.31's 3x order pre-flight fan-out hitting an unchanged 500/s bucket; disabling the feature flag fixed it while preserving the limit (no quota was loosened).

Reasoning: Rejecting 90% at 80 rps against a 600/s cap right after a config migration smells like a unit or interval mistranslation in the new file. Read the live token-bucket params the limiter actually loaded.
<|call:inspect_token_bucket|> {"service":"payments-api"}
System Response: {"capacity":600,"refill_tokens":600,"refill_interval":"1m"}
Recovery reasoning: Found it: refill_interval is 1m, not 1s. The bucket refills 600 tokens per MINUTE = 10/s effective, so 80 rps drains it instantly. The old YAML was per-second. Confirm the migration intent before editing.
<|call:get_redis_key|> {"key":"limits:payments-api:source"}
System Response: legacy YAML rate=600 period=second; new TOML mapped period -> refill_interval as "1m" (bare interval defaulted to minutes)
Recovery reasoning: The TOML loader defaulted a bare interval to minutes; the original spec is per-second. Patch refill_interval to 1s to restore the documented 600/s.
<|call:set_limiter_config|> {"service":"payments-api","field":"refill_interval","value":"1s"}
System Response: applied; refill 600 tokens/1s; reject rate 90%->0.0% at 80 rps; headroom restored
Resolution: Root cause was the TOML migration defaulting refill_interval to minutes (10/s effective) versus the intended 600/s; setting it to 1s restored capacity without raising the documented limit.

Reasoning: A few 429s snowballing into an outage is classic retry amplification -- the client ignores Retry-After and retries immediately, so each throttle multiplies load. First confirm the client is configured to honor the header.
<|call:set_retry_policy|> {"service":"notification-worker","action":"inspect"}
System Response: {"strategy":"fixed","base_delay_ms":200,"max_retries":5,"respect_retry_after":true}
Recovery reasoning: Trap -- config claims respect_retry_after=true, so on paper we obey the header, yet the outage says otherwise. The header may be read but its value dropped. Tail logs around a throttle event to see the backoff actually used.
<|call:tail_logs|> {"service":"notification-worker","filter":"429 OR retry-after","lines":40}
System Response: "429 received Retry-After=30s" then 5 retries at 200ms,400ms,800ms... header value never applied; only base_delay used
Recovery reasoning: The flag is read but the parser only handles the HTTP-date form of Retry-After, not delta-seconds ("30"), so it silently falls back to base_delay. Need delta-seconds honored plus a cap so retries cannot outlast the window.
<|call:set_retry_policy|> {"service":"notification-worker","strategy":"exponential","respect_retry_after":true,"parse_delta_seconds":true,"max_retries":3}
System Response: deployed; next 429 worker sleeps 30s per header; provider 429s cleared, no snowball
Resolution: Root cause was the retry client parsing only HTTP-date Retry-After and ignoring delta-seconds, so it hammered through the 30s window; honoring delta-seconds plus capped exponential backoff stopped the amplification.

Reasoning: ~2x the limit clustering at the minute boundary is the textbook fixed-window edge: a client sends 100 at :59 and 100 at :01, counted in different windows. Confirm which algorithm the limiter uses.
<|call:inspect_rate_limiter|> {"service":"api-gateway","plan":"100rpm"}
System Response: {"algorithm":"fixed_window","window":"60s","limit":100,"status":"within_limit"}
Recovery reasoning: Trap -- every individual window reports 'within_limit', which hides that fixed_window resets the counter to 0 each calendar minute, so straddling the boundary allows ~200 in a short burst. Verify the boundary burst in the counter history.
<|call:get_redis_key|> {"key":"ratelimit:fixed:cust_8842","fields":"window_start,count"}
System Response: window 12:30:00 count=98 (last at 12:30:59); window 12:31:00 count=92 (last at 12:31:04) -> 190 in ~5s
Recovery reasoning: Boundary doubling confirmed. Switch this plan to a sliding window so any rolling 60s span is capped at 100, eliminating the edge without lowering the advertised limit.
<|call:set_limiter_config|> {"service":"api-gateway","plan":"100rpm","algorithm":"sliding_window","window":"60s","limit":100}
System Response: applied; rolling 60s enforcement; cust_8842 capped at 100 per any 60s; boundary burst gone
Resolution: Root cause was fixed-window reset allowing a 2x burst across the minute boundary; switching to a sliding window enforces a rolling 100/60s and removes the edge while keeping the same advertised limit.

Reasoning: Identical keys decided differently across pods, with oscillating throughput and no deploy, could be a corrupt shared counter or per-pod time skew. Start at the shared Redis counter since every pod reads it.
<|call:get_redis_key|> {"key":"ratelimit:gw:global","fields":"count,ttl"}
System Response: {"count":312,"ttl":41,"status":"OK"} -- counter consistent, single source of truth healthy
Recovery reasoning: Trap -- the shared counter is healthy and singular, so it is not counter corruption. If every pod reads the same count but decides differently, the difference must be in how each pod computes the window, i.e. local time. Check clock sync across the pods.
<|call:check_clock_sync|> {"group":"gateway","pods":"all"}
System Response: gateway-0..4 offset <5ms; gateway-5 offset +2150ms (chrony/NTP daemon dead since 06:12)
Recovery reasoning: gateway-5 is +2.15s, so its floor(local_ts/60) window id flips early near boundaries and admits or rejects out of phase with peers reading the same counter. Resync its clock.
<|call:restart_service|> {"target":"chrony","pod":"gateway-5"}
System Response: chrony restarted; gateway-5 offset +2150ms->3ms; oscillation flat; cross-pod decisions consistent
Resolution: Root cause was a dead chrony on gateway-5 skewing it +2.15s so it computed window ids out of phase despite a healthy shared counter; resyncing the clock restored consistent distributed enforcement (no limit changed).

Reasoning: Everyone getting 429s suggests the global bucket is exhausted. First check the ingestion bucket's health to see if the limit itself regressed.
<|call:inspect_rate_limiter|> {"bucket":"ingestion"}
System Response: {"limit":10000,"window":"60s","utilization":"100%","status":"healthy","errors":0}
Recovery reasoning: Trap -- the limiter reports 'healthy' with zero errors because it is doing exactly what it is configured to do: cap at 10k. That hides a fairness problem: 100% utilization while everyone else starves means one key is hogging the pool. Rank consumers.
<|call:list_top_consumers|> {"bucket":"ingestion","window":"5m","top":5}
System Response: acme-prod (key ...7f3a) 9.4k/10k rpm = 94%; remaining 4 tenants share 6%
Recovery reasoning: acme-prod eats 94% of a shared pool with no per-tenant cap. Raising the global limit just lets it eat more; the right fix is a per-tenant ceiling so no single key can starve the rest.
<|call:set_limiter_config|> {"bucket":"ingestion","per_tenant_limit":3000,"window":"60s"}
System Response: applied; acme-prod capped at 3k rpm; other tenants P99 timeouts->85ms; global 10k ceiling unchanged
Resolution: Root cause was a shared global bucket with no per-tenant ceiling letting acme-prod consume 94% and starve others; a 3k rpm per-tenant cap restored fairness while preserving the 10k global invariant.

Reasoning: A 5000 burst on a 500/s steady rate means a synchronized client wave can legally fire 5000 requests instantly -- the limiter permits it but downstream cannot absorb it. First confirm it is the burst being drained, not the steady rate.
<|call:inspect_token_bucket|> {"service":"search-api"}
System Response: {"capacity":5000,"refill_rate":500,"refill_interval":"1s","current_tokens":4980,"status":"matches_spec"}
Recovery reasoning: Trap -- the bucket matches our documented spec exactly, so nothing is 'broken', yet the spec itself is the bug: it sits near-full (4980) between waves, so each cron herd can spend ~5000 tokens in one burst. Quantify the downstream limit before resizing.
<|call:query_metrics|> {"metric":"es_query_queue_depth","service":"elasticsearch","window":"last_herd"}
System Response: queue cap 1000; herd drove depth to 4900 -> rejections; sustainable inflow ~800 concurrent
Recovery reasoning: Elasticsearch tolerates ~1000 queued but our burst lets 5000 through. Shrink burst to ~1000 (just above steady) so spikes smooth out but the herd cannot exceed downstream capacity; keep refill at 500/s.
<|call:set_limiter_config|> {"service":"search-api","field":"capacity","value":1000}
System Response: applied; burst capped at 1000; next cron herd queued at 980 depth, no ES rejections; steady 500/s intact
Resolution: Root cause was an oversized 5000-token burst exceeding Elasticsearch's ~1000 queue capacity during synchronized cron waves; shrinking the burst to 1000 smoothed spikes without touching the 500/s steady rate.

Reasoning: A breaker that half-opens, sends one probe, and re-opens repeatedly usually means the half-open trial is too strict -- one trial cannot satisfy the close condition. Since thresholds were tuned yesterday, inspect the breaker config first.
<|call:inspect_circuit_breaker|> {"service":"inventory-svc"}
System Response: {"state":"half_open","half_open_max_calls":1,"success_threshold":5,"error":"cannot reach success_threshold=5 with max_calls=1"}
Recovery reasoning: Contradiction surfaced: half_open_max_calls=1 but success_threshold=5 -- it can never accumulate 5 successes from a single trial call, so it can never close. Confirm downstream is genuinely healthy before loosening anything.
<|call:query_metrics|> {"metric":"inventory_svc_success_rate","window":"5m"}
System Response: direct probes 99.7% success, p99 60ms -- downstream fully recovered; only the breaker is shedding
Recovery reasoning: Downstream is healthy and the breaker math is impossible. Raise half_open_max_calls so a recovered dependency can actually reach success_threshold and close.
<|call:set_breaker_thresholds|> {"service":"inventory-svc","half_open_max_calls":10,"success_threshold":5}
System Response: applied; breaker accumulated 10/10 successes; state half_open->closed; shedding 70%->0%
Resolution: Root cause was yesterday's tuning leaving half_open_max_calls(1) below success_threshold(5), making closure mathematically impossible; raising trial calls to 10 let the recovered dependency close the breaker.

Reasoning: OOM with millions of queued messages points to an unbounded in-memory queue absorbing backpressure instead of rejecting it. Inspect the queue between handler and writer first.
<|call:inspect_queue|> {"service":"ingest-pipeline","queue":"handler_to_writer"}
System Response: {"type":"unbounded_channel","depth":2140000,"capacity":"unlimited","oldest_age":"19m"}
Recovery reasoning: Confirmed -- an unbounded channel at 2.1M depth. The intake rate limit caps arrivals but the DB writer is slower than intake, so the gap accumulates in RAM until OOM. Measure the writer's true drain rate to size a bound.
<|call:query_metrics|> {"metric":"db_writer_throughput","service":"ingest-pipeline","window":"10m"}
System Response: writer sustains 1.2k msg/s; intake admits 3k msg/s -> 1.8k/s accumulates with nowhere to go
Recovery reasoning: Intake (3k/s) outpaces the writer (1.2k/s) by 1.8k/s, so any unbounded queue must OOM. Bound the queue so it applies backpressure (rejects at the handler) instead of buffering forever, and align intake admission to the drain rate.
<|call:set_queue_policy|> {"service":"ingest-pipeline","queue_capacity":20000,"on_full":"reject_429","intake_rate":1200}
System Response: applied; queue bounded at 20k, returns 429 when full; intake throttled to 1.2k/s; heap flat, OOMKills stopped
Resolution: Root cause was an unbounded handler-to-writer channel buffering the 1.8k/s gap between intake and the DB writer until OOM; a bounded queue with 429-on-full backpressure plus intake matched to the 1.2k/s drain rate stopped the kills.

Reasoning: A limiter that intermittently forgets limits, correlated with Redis near maxmemory, smells like the eviction policy deleting limiter counters. Check the Redis maxmemory policy first.
<|call:get_redis_key|> {"key":"CONFIG","field":"maxmemory-policy"}
System Response: {"maxmemory-policy":"allkeys-lru","maxmemory":"4gb","used_memory":"3.98gb","evicted_keys_last_hour":120400}
Recovery reasoning: There it is: allkeys-lru evicts ANY key under pressure, including limiter counters. When a client's counter is evicted mid-window, the next request rebuilds it from zero, resetting their quota. Confirm a limiter key was actually evicted at the incident time.
<|call:tail_logs|> {"service":"redis","filter":"evicted ratelimit:","lines":20}
System Response: 02:00:14 evicted ratelimit:sliding:client_55 (idle 9s) -- counter rebuilt from 0 on next req, allowing the full 1000 again
Recovery reasoning: Limiter keys are being evicted as collateral. Switch the policy to volatile-lru (only evict keys carrying a deliberate TTL) and protect the ratelimit namespace from eviction, so caches reclaim but counters cannot.
<|call:set_redis_policy|> {"maxmemory-policy":"volatile-lru","protect_namespace":"ratelimit:","action":"no_evict"}
System Response: applied; ratelimit:* excluded from eviction; cache keys still reclaimable; client_55 now capped at 1000/min
Resolution: Root cause was Redis allkeys-lru evicting sliding-window counters under memory pressure and resetting quotas to zero; switching to volatile-lru and protecting the ratelimit: namespace stopped the over-admission while keeping cache eviction working.

Reasoning: Available concurrency slowly bleeding to zero while CPU is idle is a permit leak -- slots acquired but never released, usually on an error or timeout path. Inspect the semaphore state first.
<|call:get_semaphore_state|> {"service":"pdf-render-svc"}
System Response: {"max_permits":50,"available":3,"held":47,"held_over_5min":44,"longest_held":"2h11m"}
Recovery reasoning: 44 permits held over 5 minutes (one for 2h+) on a render that should take seconds -- those are leaked, never released. Find the path that acquires but does not release; tail logs for render timeouts.
<|call:tail_logs|> {"service":"pdf-render-svc","filter":"timeout OR panic render","lines":30}
System Response: repeated "render timeout after 30s" with no matching "permit released" -- release only runs on the happy-path return, skipped on timeout/panic
Recovery reasoning: Permits release only on a successful return, so every timeout or panic leaks one. Move release into a deferred/finally guard so it always runs, and reclaim the already-leaked permits without a full restart.
<|call:set_concurrency_policy|> {"service":"pdf-render-svc","release_mode":"deferred_finally","reclaim_stale_permits_older_than":"60s"}
System Response: applied; 44 stale permits reclaimed; available 3->47; release now runs on all exit paths; throughput restored, no restart
Resolution: Root cause was the concurrency limiter releasing permits only on the happy path, leaking one per render timeout/panic until exhaustion; a finally-guarded release plus stale-permit reclaim restored the 50-slot pool without a restart.

Reasoning: A per-pod limit of 500 rps 'looks correct' but with N pods the effective ceiling is N*500. After scaling 4->16 that is 8000 rps against a 2000 agreement. First confirm each pod's local limit is in fact per-instance, not global.
<|call:inspect_rate_limiter|> {"service":"checkout-api","target":"tax-svc"}
System Response: {"limit":500,"scope":"per_instance","status":"healthy","per_pod_compliant":true}
Recovery reasoning: Trap -- every pod is individually 'compliant' at 500 rps and reports healthy, but it is a per-instance scope with no global coordination, so 16 pods = 8000 rps. Confirm the replica count that is multiplying it.
<|call:check_autoscaler|> {"deployment":"checkout-api"}
System Response: HPA scaled 4->16 replicas at 11:40 (sale traffic); 16 * 500 = 8000 rps to tax-svc vs 2000 agreed
Recovery reasoning: Per-instance limits do not compose under autoscaling. Switch to a global distributed limiter (shared Redis token bucket) keyed on the tax-svc target so the 2000 rps cap holds regardless of replica count.
<|call:set_limiter_config|> {"service":"checkout-api","target":"tax-svc","scope":"global_distributed","backend":"redis","limit":2000}
System Response: applied; cluster-wide cap 2000 rps shared across all 16 pods; tax-svc inflow 8000->1980 rps; shedding stopped
Resolution: Root cause was a per-instance 500 rps limit that multiplied to 8000 rps when the HPA scaled to 16 pods; moving to a global Redis-backed limiter enforces the 2000 rps agreement independent of replica count.

Reasoning: Expensive nested queries showing the same cost as trivial ones means the cost-weighting is not accounting for query complexity -- every request is billed a flat weight. Inspect how the limiter computes cost.
<|call:inspect_rate_limiter|> {"service":"graphql-gateway","field":"cost_function"}
System Response: {"cost_function":"flat","weight_per_request":1,"budget":10000,"note":"depth/multiplier fields present but unused"}
Recovery reasoning: Found it: cost is flat (1 unit/request), so a 5000-resolver nested query costs the same as a health check. The depth multiplier exists but is not wired in, so heavy clients sit at 30% budget while crushing the DB. Quantify the real cost of the offending queries.
<|call:query_trace|> {"service":"graphql-gateway","filter":"client_top_db_time","window":"10m"}
System Response: client_19 avg depth 7, 4200 resolver calls/req, 900ms DB time -- billed 1 unit each (true cost ~400)
Recovery reasoning: Real cost is ~400x the flat weight. Enable complexity-based cost (depth * resolver-count) so heavy queries draw down budget proportionally, keeping the 10000/min ceiling but making it meaningful.
<|call:set_limiter_config|> {"service":"graphql-gateway","cost_function":"complexity","weight":"depth*resolver_count","budget":10000}
System Response: applied; client_19 nested query now costs ~410 units; client throttled at true budget; DB load -75%
Resolution: Root cause was a flat 1-unit cost function ignoring query complexity, letting nested queries hammer the DB while showing 30% budget; enabling depth*resolver complexity weighting made the 10000/min budget reflect real cost.

Reasoning: Latency climbing while nothing drops, with a leaky bucket always full, means the leak (drain) rate is below inflow -- requests queue in the bucket instead of being rejected. Inspect the leaky-bucket parameters.
<|call:inspect_token_bucket|> {"service":"email-dispatch","type":"leaky_bucket"}
System Response: {"bucket_size":50000,"leak_rate":200,"inflow_avg":600,"current_depth":49800,"drops":0}
Recovery reasoning: Inflow is 600/s but leak_rate is only 200/s, so the bucket fills and every message waits behind ~50k others (~250s worst case) -- that is the latency, not drops. The big bucket merely hid the imbalance as queue delay. Check the relay's real sustainable rate before raising the leak.
<|call:query_metrics|> {"metric":"smtp_relay_accept_rate","service":"smtp-relay","window":"10m"}
System Response: relay comfortably accepts 550/s (we throttled ourselves to 200/s); 600/s inflow is sustainable downstream
Recovery reasoning: The relay can take 550/s but our leak is throttled to 200/s, so the backlog is self-inflicted. Raise leak_rate to ~550 to match downstream capacity and shrink the oversized bucket so excess sheds quickly instead of queuing for minutes.
<|call:set_limiter_config|> {"service":"email-dispatch","leak_rate":550,"bucket_size":5000}
System Response: applied; leak 550/s clears inflow; depth 49800->1200; e2e latency 90s->2.4s; still zero drops
Resolution: Root cause was a leak_rate (200/s) far below inflow (600/s) plus an oversized bucket converting the imbalance into 90s of queue latency; raising the leak to the relay's sustainable 550/s and shrinking the bucket restored 2s latency without dropping messages.
